Boost 1.72 mpl: give integral_c a step helper that is safe for enums.

mpl::integral_c<T,N> unconditionally defines

    next  = integral_c<T, static_cast<T>(N + 1)>
    prior = integral_c<T, static_cast<T>(N - 1)>

as member typedefs, so both are instantiated whenever the class is, whether
or not anyone asks for them. When T is an enumeration without a fixed
underlying type, static_cast<T> of a value outside the enumeration's range is
undefined behaviour, and undefined behaviour is not a constant expression --
so the non-type template argument is ill-formed. C++ has always said so;
clang only began enforcing it, and from clang 20 the -Wno-enum-constexpr-
conversion escape hatch no longer suppresses the resulting hard error.

This half of the fix adds the helper. boost::mpl::aux::wrapper_step<T> steps
in the ordinary way for arithmetic T, and is specialised for enumerations to
report the value unchanged: stepping an enumeration is not meaningful, the
typedefs exist only because the class template defines them unconditionally,
and nothing in Boost or in this tree reads integral_c<Enum,N>::next/prior.

Guarded on C++11, which is where constexpr is available; below that nothing
is defined and integral_wrapper.hpp keeps its original code path verbatim.

